=== Relocation to Asakusa === In 1657, the [[:en:Great Fire of Meireki|Great Fire of Meireki]] devastated Edo, prompting the relocation of Yoshiwara to the outskirts of Asakusa. This new location, encircled by moats and accessible by a single official pathway, symbolized its isolation as a "pleasure district" separate from the city. The district's expansion increased its area to three chō (about 330 meters square), accommodating more establishments.
=== Peak and Decline === During its heyday, Yoshiwara was a cultural hub for fashion, arts, and literature, influencing trends throughout Edo. The district's courtesans, known as [[:en:Oiran|Oiran]], were renowned for their elaborate costumes and parades. However, competition from unlicensed brothels (岡場所, *okabasho*) and economic changes began eroding its prominence by the late [[:en:Edo period|Edo period]].
In the [[:en:Meiji era|Meiji era]], the rise of [[:en:Geisha|Geisha]] districts and societal modernization reduced Yoshiwara's appeal as a center of elite entertainment. The district was destroyed during the [[:en:Great Kantō earthquake|Great Kantō earthquake]] in 1923 and again in the [[:en:Bombing of Tokyo|Bombing of Tokyo]] in 1945.
=== Closure === Following the enactment of the [[Prostitution Prevention Law]] in 1958, Yoshiwara officially ceased operations as a licensed red-light district. Many former establishments transitioned to other forms of entertainment or specialty baths.
== Cultural Contributions == Yoshiwara played a central role in Edo's cultural development. It inspired numerous [[ukiyo-e]] prints, including works by [[Utagawa Hiroshige]] and [[:en:Kitagawa Utamaro|Kitagawa Utamaro]]. Its architecture, fashion, and rituals influenced broader Japanese aesthetics. Yoshiwara was also a setting for kabuki theater and literary works, symbolizing both decadence and refined culture.
== Legacy == Today, the area formerly known as Yoshiwara exists as part of modern-day [[Taitō]], Tokyo, with remnants of its historical significance preserved in literature, art, and cultural memory. == See Also == * [[Tokugawa period]] * [[Edo culture]] * [[Prostitution in Japan]] * [[Ukiyo-e]]
