'''Yoshiwara Yūkaku''' (吉原遊廓) was a licensed red-light district in [[:en:Edo|Edo]] (modern-day [[Tokyo]]) officially sanctioned by the [[Tokugawa shogunate]]. Initially located in Nihonbashi, it was later relocated to [[Asakusa]] following the [[:en:Great Fire of Meireki|Great Fire of Meireki]] in 1657. The district became known as '''Moto Yoshiwara''' (Old Yoshiwara) and '''Shin Yoshiwara''' (New Yoshiwara) after the move.
== History == === Establishment and Moto Yoshiwara === [[Tokugawa Ieyasu]] established Edo as the seat of power in 1590, leading to rapid urban growth and an imbalance in the male-to-female ratio. The shogunate approved the establishment of Yoshiwara in 1617 to regulate prostitution and centralize such activities. Yoshiwara was initially located near Nihonbashi, named after the marshlands ("yoshi" means reed) in the area.
=== Relocation to Asakusa === In 1657, the [[:en:Great Fire of Meireki|Great Fire of Meireki]] devastated Edo, prompting the relocation of Yoshiwara to the outskirts of Asakusa. This new location, encircled by moats and accessible by a single official pathway, symbolized its isolation as a "pleasure district" separate from the city. The district's expansion increased its area to three chō (about 330 meters square), accommodating more establishments.
=== Peak and Decline === During its heyday, Yoshiwara was a cultural hub for fashion, arts, and literature, influencing trends throughout Edo. The district's courtesans, known as [[:en:Oiran|Oiran]], were renowned for their elaborate costumes and parades. However, competition from unlicensed brothels (岡場所, *okabasho*) and economic changes began eroding its prominence by the late [[:en:Edo period|Edo period]].
In the [[:en:Meiji era|Meiji era]], the rise of [[:en:Geisha|Geisha]] districts and societal modernization reduced Yoshiwara's appeal as a center of elite entertainment. The district was destroyed during the [[:en:Great Kantō earthquake|Great Kantō earthquake]] in 1923 and again in the [[:en:Bombing of Tokyo|Bombing of Tokyo]] in 1945.
=== Closure === Following the enactment of the [[:en:Prostitution Prevention Law|Prostitution Prevention Law]] in 1958, Yoshiwara officially ceased operations as a licensed red-light district. Many former establishments transitioned to other forms of entertainment or specialty baths.
== Cultural Contributions == Yoshiwara played a central role in Edo's cultural development. It inspired numerous [[ukiyo-e]] prints, including works by [[:en:Utagawa Hiroshige|Utagawa Hiroshige]] and [[:en:Kitagawa Utamaro|Kitagawa Utamaro]]. Its architecture, fashion, and rituals influenced broader Japanese aesthetics. Yoshiwara was also a setting for kabuki theater and literary works, symbolizing both decadence and refined culture.
== Legacy == Today, the area formerly known as Yoshiwara exists as part of modern-day [[:en:Taitō|Taitō]], Tokyo, with remnants of its historical significance preserved in literature, art, and cultural memory. == See Also == * [[Tokugawa period]] * [[Edo culture]] * [[:en:Prostitution in Japan|Prostitution in Japan]] * [[Ukiyo-e]]
